## TRAPS IN THE CLAIMS (the drafter's reading at `e8f5208`; each is a READING you measure)
- **T-a (-281, the decoder is a table, measured on ONE runtime).** `namesFiles` (`formGate.ts:175-187` @ `e8f5208`) matches `^=\?([^?]*)\?[Bb]\?([^?]*)\?=$` (`:176`), looks the charset up in `MIME_CHARSETS` (36 names, `:158-166`), strips only `[ \t\r\n]` from the data (`:179`), requires `length % 4 === 0` and `^[A-Za-z0-9+/]*={0,2}$` (`:180`), decodes with `atob` (`:181`) and compares bytes to `files` / `"files"` in the charset's width and byte order (`:183-186`); anything it cannot decode returns `null` and `isFilePart` counts it (`:149`, fail toward counting). **B267 measured .NET's decoding on ASP.NET Core 10.0.13 only** (`:55-60`; its `formGate.ts:156` comment says so). The hosted API runs **10.0.12** today (B269 is measuring a move: OPEN until its last line) and CI's Linux runner 10.0.13. The table and every rule (letter case of `B`, the 5-piece split, padding, whitespace skipped, BOM kept, quotes removed) is a claim about the API's reader on the runtime the API actually runs. **R-3.** A difference between 10.0.12 and 10.0.13 on any row = a finding (severity by B257's bar).
- **T-b (-281, the BFF decodes header bytes as windows-1252; the API as … UNMEASURED).** `isFilePart` is fed `new TextDecoder("latin1").decode(…)` (`:281`), which WHATWG maps to windows-1252 (bytes 0x80-0x9F are not Latin-1 there). How .NET's `MultipartReader` decodes header bytes is UNMEASURED at drafting. And header NAMES: the BFF matches `/^content-disposition[ \t]*:[ \t]*/i` (`:144`, a JS regex without `u`, whose `/i` never folds a non-ASCII letter to ASCII), while .NET looks a section's headers up by name with a comparer whose behaviour on `ı` (U+0131 → `I`) and `ſ` (U+017F → `S`) is UNMEASURED. **Drafter's prime bypass candidate (UNMEASURED):** a part whose header name is `Content-Dıſposıtıon` (UTF-8 bytes) with `form-data; name="files"; filename="a.png"`: if .NET reads it as the Content-Disposition and the BFF does not, the part is a file the BFF never counts. Measure it (Check 2c) both ways, and with the same spelling in `name`'s parameter NAME (`nAme` is ASCII-folded by both; `nаme` with Cyrillic `а` U+0430 is not a token).
- **T-c (-281, the 413 rows prove one spelling).** The Done-when's `handleApi` row is ONE spelling (`=?utf-8?B?ZmlsZXM=?=`, `bff.formstream.test.ts:156-163` @ `e8f5208`). B267 measured 0 bypass lines in B263's corpus (`:66`), whose encoded-word lines B263 generated from a handful of atoms. The decoder's edges (Check 2b) are where a bypass would live: a row the BFF reads as NOT files (returns `false`, not `null`) while .NET reads `files`. `false` is returned only on a well-formed word in a listed charset whose bytes are not `files`/`"files"` (`:183-186`), or a non-word name ≠ `files` (`:177`). Attack exactly that set.
- **T-d (-285, the instrument is blind to most of the work).** The Done-when's test (`formMeter.test.ts:315-372` @ `e8f5208`) spies `Uint8Array.prototype.indexOf` and sums the bytes each call can examine (`:324-329`), and counts `new Uint8Array(n)` allocations by stubbing the global (`:330-336`). It does NOT see: `indexOfBytes`' inner compare loop (`formGate.ts:199-200`: up to `delim.length` = boundary + 4 bytes per candidate, 74 at the 70-character limit), `held.set(chunk, len)` (`:233`), and **`held.copyWithin(0, i, len)` on every chunk (`:285`)**. In the "headers" state with no line break found, `i` is 0 (`:236`, `:269-273`), so each byte-sized chunk runs `copyWithin(0, 0, len)` over everything held: O(held) per chunk unless the engine skips a same-place copy (UNMEASURED). B267's wall-clock re-run of `quad2f.mjs` (ratios 1.41-2.11 at tip, `:111-114`) is the only measure that sees it, and 2.11 is near its own 2.2 bar. **Measure** with a second instrument that counts bytes MOVED (`copyWithin`, `set`) and bytes COMPARED (a scratch copy of `indexOfBytes` with a counter, applied in `-mut` only, never as proof of the product), and by wall time at 16, 32 and 48 KiB held (one-byte header lines `a\r\n` reach 3 × 16 KiB held: the memory bound's own worst case, `formGate.ts:194`).
- **T-e (-284, the limit was measured on clean lines).** B267's `dnlimit` (`probes/dnlimit-Program.cs`) swept 1-3 header lines, sums 16,360-16,400, reads of 1 B to 1 MiB, and found ".NET reads a block iff its header lines without line breaks total ≤ 16,383 bytes" (`:89-91`). The BFF gives up at `lines + len - pos - (buf[len - 1] === 13 ? 1 : 0) >= MAX_PART_HEADERS` (`formGate.ts:272`) and at `lines >= MAX_PART_HEADERS` (`:278`). UNMEASURED by either: a bare CR or a bare LF inside a line (counted as a header byte here; by .NET?), LF-only line ends, `CR CR LF`, a CR that ends a chunk and is NOT followed by LF (`:272`'s subtraction), the API's **`HeadersCountLimit = 16`** (`api/src/HpsmPoc.Modules.Feedback/Endpoints/FeedbackFiles.cs:300` @ `e8f5208`: the BFF does not count lines, so a 17-line block is read here and refused there), obs-fold at the limit, and the delimiter line's padding (B267 F-4: .NET refuses ≥ 100 bytes; the BFF holds up to 16 KiB, `:259`). Any block .NET READS that the BFF gives up on (in any chunking) = an uncounted file part after it = a bypass.
